### Changes proposed in this Pull Request:

This adds an endpoint to facilitate disconnecting all services at once:

```
DELETE /wp-json/wc/gla/connections
```

This endpoint is the equivalent of calling all of these endpoints in succession:

1. `DELETE /wp-json/wc/gla/ads/connection`
1. `DELETE /wp-json/wc/gla/mc/connection`
1. `DELETE /wp-json/wc/gla/google/connect`
1. `DELETE /wp-json/wc/gla/jetpack/connect`

The response data will be an object with two top-level keys, `errors` and `responses`. Each will be an object keyed with the route, and the value will be an object with the response details. E.g.

```json
{
    "errors": [],
    "responses": {
        "\/wc\/gla\/ads\/connection": {
            "status": "success",
            "message": "Successfully disconnected."
        },
        "\/wc\/gla\/mc\/connection": {
            "status": "success",
            "message": "Successfully disconnected."
        },
        "\/wc\/gla\/google\/connect": {
            "status": "success",
            "message": "Successfully disconnected."
        },
        "\/wc\/gla\/jetpack\/connect": {
            "status": "success",
            "message": "Successfully disconnected."
        }
    }
}
```

If *any* of the responses generate an error, this endpoint will send a `400` status. If there are no errors, then a `200` status will be returned.

Helps #286.

### Detailed test instructions:

1. Make a `DELETE` request to `/wp-json/wc/gla/connections`. No body is required.
2. Check the response to see that all connections were disconnected and no errors were generated
3. Check the connection test page to see that there are no connections established
